1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,724
Section ten.

2
00:00:02,004 --> 00:00:06,505
Today the fact table is rebuilt from all trips on every run, and that gets slow.

3
00:00:06,785 --> 00:00:10,091
We make it incremental, so only new trips are processed.

4
00:00:10,372 --> 00:00:12,548
Then a new month arrives, with a surprise.

5
00:00:13,648 --> 00:00:16,613
A table is thrown away and rebuilt on every run.

6
00:00:16,893 --> 00:00:25,192
An incremental model keeps the existing table and processes only new rows, inserting them, or replacing rows with the same key.

7
00:00:26,291 --> 00:00:28,787
The incremental models page, version one.

8
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:34,230
The is incremental function is true only on a normal run against an existing table.

9
00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:37,049
The word this refers to the existing table.

10
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:40,102
A unique key lets dbt replace rows.

11
00:00:40,382 --> 00:00:44,030
And on schema change decides what happens when columns change.

12
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:46,154
The whole pattern.

13
00:00:46,434 --> 00:00:48,546
A config block that says incremental.

14
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:50,832
Then an if block that adds a filter.

15
00:00:51,112 --> 00:00:54,141
On the first run, or a full refresh, everything loads.

16
00:00:54,421 --> 00:00:58,197
On later runs, only rows newer than what is already in the table.

17
00:00:59,297 --> 00:01:00,641
How are new rows written?

18
00:01:00,921 --> 00:01:03,822
The DuckDB configs page lists four strategies.

19
00:01:04,102 --> 00:01:07,260
Append, delete and insert, merge, and microbatch.

20
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:13,406
We use delete and insert with our trip ID: simple, and safe when the same trip arrives twice.

21
00:01:14,506 --> 00:01:18,346
Before changing anything, we ask in Ask mode how this would behave.

22
00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:26,456
A trip from the last days of the previous month could be missed, so we look back a few days, and the unique key replaces anything we see twice.

23
00:01:27,556 --> 00:01:29,902
How many days back is a setting, not code.

24
00:01:30,182 --> 00:01:33,916
So it becomes a project variable, the vars key from section two.

25
00:01:34,196 --> 00:01:37,332
We can override it on the command line for a single run.

26
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:47,520
In the demo, the agent makes the fact table incremental: the trip ID as unique key, delete and insert, and a filter that uses our lookback variable.

27
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:52,258
We leave on schema change at its default, and run it once with a full refresh.

28
00:01:53,358 --> 00:01:54,318
We review it.

29
00:01:54,598 --> 00:02:01,212
The folder says table, but the config in the file says incremental, and the file wins because it is more specific.

30
00:02:01,492 --> 00:02:06,121
The validity filter, the lookback from the variable, and no on schema change yet.

31
00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:08,650
Now real life happens.

32
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:11,234
January twenty twenty-five is published.

33
00:02:11,514 --> 00:02:14,288
We add the file to our load script and run it again.

34
00:02:14,568 --> 00:02:21,650
Then dbt build for staging and everything downstream, and Run History shows that only the new rows were processed.

35
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:23,753
The surprise.

36
00:02:24,033 --> 00:02:32,652
January brings a new column: the C B D congestion fee, for the Manhattan Congestion Relief Zone that started on January fifth.

37
00:02:32,932 --> 00:02:36,537
It is in the raw table, but not in staging or the fact table.

38
00:02:36,817 --> 00:02:39,612
Nothing changes downstream without our decision.

39
00:02:40,712 --> 00:02:41,842
Back to the docs.

40
00:02:42,122 --> 00:02:44,810
The default, ignore, does not add new columns.

41
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:46,754
Append new columns adds them.

42
00:02:47,034 --> 00:02:49,232
And none of the options fill in old rows.

43
00:02:49,512 --> 00:02:50,962
That needs a full refresh.

44
00:02:52,062 --> 00:03:05,417
In the demo, the agent carries the new column through every layer: the dictionary file, the source, staging, intermediate and the fact table, and sets on schema change so the column is added without a full refresh.

45
00:03:05,697 --> 00:03:09,110
Updating the dictionary keeps the agent's context current.

46
00:03:10,210 --> 00:03:11,362
The column appears.

47
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:17,167
January rows have values, November and December are empty, exactly as the docs said.

48
00:03:17,447 --> 00:03:22,119
Here empty is actually correct, because the fee did not exist before January.

49
00:03:22,399 --> 00:03:24,128
Know which option you are choosing.
